<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slutsk</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slutsk is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> Region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, south of Minsk. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olelkovich">Olelkovich</a> princes and later of the Radziwiłłs, who made it a fortified town with schools and churches. In the eighteenth century its workshops produced the famous Slutsk sashes, woven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> belts worn by the Polish-Lithuanian nobility. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-partition-of-poland">Second Partition of Poland</a> in 1793.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slutsk.jpg" alt="Slutsk" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: скульптор Сергей Оганов и архитектор Сергей Багласов · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slutsk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
